/scripts/.wikimedia-cache.json

# Google Flights poller OAuth files (DO NOT COMMIT)
scanner/gflights_credentials.json
scanner/gflights_token.json

# Hermes 2026-10-27: runtime data files.
#
# The app is self-hosted on the NAS (Next.js standalone on 127.0.0.1:3000,
# fronted by a Cloudflare Tunnel). The Mac-side poller pushes these JSON
# files to the NAS via scanner/push_data_to_nas.sh, and the running server
# reads them from /volume1/flight-scanner/ directly.
#
# Previously they were committed to git, and every commit triggered a
# Vercel GitHub auto-deploy — ~27 deploys/day purely to move a data file,
# which is what burned the Hobby quota. Now the data path is:
#
#   poller → scp to NAS → running app reads it from disk (5s cache)
#
# No git, no commit, no deploy. The Vercel project's Git link has also
# been disconnected (link: null, createDeployments: disabled) so a stray
# push can never auto-deploy again.
#
# IMPORTANT: these files are still TRACKED in git, and must stay tracked.
# Six components statically import them at build time:
#   src/app/page.tsx           → all_dates.json, all_dates_szx.json
#   src/app/route/[code]/page.tsx → all_dates.json, all_dates_szx.json
#   src/app/trump/page.tsx      → trump_alerts.json
#   src/app/trump/serenity-tabs.tsx → serenity_data.json
#   src/app/worldcup/page.tsx   → worldcup_latest.json
# Untracking them breaks `npm run build` on a clean checkout. They are
# committed to git as build inputs, NOT as the live data path — the live
# path is the NAS copy, which the app reads at runtime.
#
# To stop the hourly cron from committing them (it no longer does — see
# scanner/gflights_poll_and_deploy.sh), untrack them with:
#   git rm --cached src/data/*.json
# but then the build breaks. Leave them tracked.

# Hermes 2026-10-27: stray file from a bad shell redirect (empty).
/{row[2]}
scanner/__pycache__/
scanner/*.bak
scanner/*.unpatched.bak
